{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789487355,"nonce":"JLme/x5XoQ/nB0bXz+aLqYVRhbg=","title":"A string that dies at its boxing call is copied into the box first","message":"`$IO$string_to_dynamic` boxes a copy of the string so the box owns its data; the release analysis then frees the original right after the call. For a fresh string (a concatenation, `str(i)`, a slice) boxed and forgotten, that is an allocation, a copy and a release per string for nothing.\n\nNow the release analysis, when the one release point of a string is right after the call that boxes a copy of it, turns that call into `$IO$string_adopt_dynamic` (a new IO export: the box takes the string as it is, with the string dropper) and places no release. Only where that call is the string's sole release point, so nothing reads the string afterwards; a string with several release points, or read after boxing, is still copied. The symbol's role is `KEEPS_INTO_BOX`, so a later analysis reads the argument as kept.\n\nMeasured on the M1 Pro: bench_tokenize's string-building loop 21 to 18 ms; the split tokens are not covered (the list owns them), which is the other copy per token in that kernel.","files":null}]}